A direct scan is enough here. I check each value from left to right and return the first index that matches the target. If the loop finishes, the target is not in the array.

This does not use the sorted property, so it is slower than a binary search on large inputs. It is still a useful simple version to verify, and it also handles an empty array naturally.

```java
class Solution {
    public int search(int[] nums, int target) {
        for (int index = 0; index < nums.length; index++) {
            if (nums[index] == target) {
                return index;
            }
        }
        return -1;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(1)`.

The recursive helper follows the same sorted-half observation, but it passes the current boundaries instead of using a loop. At each call, it checks `mid`, finds the sorted half, and calls itself only for the possible side.

Dry run for `[6, 7, 0, 1, 2, 3, 4]` with target `3`:

- `mid` is `3`, value `1`, so the right half is sorted.
- `3` is between `1` and `4`, so the next range starts at index `4`.
- The next middle value is `3`, and index `5` is returned.

The recursion depth is logarithmic, so the call stack uses more space than the iterative versions.

```go
func search(nums []int, target int) int {
	return findTarget(nums, target, 0, len(nums)-1)
}

func findTarget(nums []int, target int, left int, right int) int {
	if left > right {
		return -1
	}

	mid := left + (right-left)/2
	if nums[mid] == target {
		return mid
	}

	if nums[left] <= nums[mid] {
		if nums[left] <= target && target < nums[mid] {
			return findTarget(nums, target, left, mid-1)
		}
		return findTarget(nums, target, mid+1, right)
	}

	if nums[mid] < target && target <= nums[right] {
		return findTarget(nums, target, mid+1, right)
	}
	return findTarget(nums, target, left, mid-1)
}
```

Time complexity: `O(log n)`.
Space complexity: `O(log n)` for the recursive call stack. The input slice is not modified.

This version keeps one ordinary binary search. The only change is how the middle value is viewed. If the middle value and target are on different sides of the rotation, the middle value is treated as either very small or very large. That prevents binary search from moving toward the wrong section.

The comparison with `nums[0]` tells which side a value belongs to. For an already sorted array, the same checks still work.

A one-element array returns either index `0` or `-1`, so no special branch is needed.

```python
class Solution:
    def search(self, nums, target):
        left = 0
        right = len(nums) - 1

        while left <= right:
            mid = left + (right - left) // 2
            middle_value = nums[mid]

            same_side = (nums[mid] >= nums[0]) == (target >= nums[0])
            if not same_side:
                if target >= nums[0]:
                    middle_value = float("inf")
                else:
                    middle_value = float("-inf")

            if middle_value == target:
                return mid
            if middle_value < target:
                left = mid + 1
            else:
                right = mid - 1

        return -1
```

Time complexity: `O(log n)`.
Space complexity: `O(1)`. No values are changed in `nums`.

I split the work into two searches. The first one finds the index of the smallest value, which is the rotation point. After that, a virtual sorted array is searched by converting each normal binary-search index to its real position with modulo.

For `[4, 5, 6, 7, 0, 1, 2]`, the rotation index is `4`. A virtual middle index is shifted by `4`, so the second search still compares values in sorted order.

An empty array is checked before using the rotation index. The array itself stays unchanged.

```javascript
class Solution {
    search(nums, target) {
        if (nums.length === 0) {
            return -1;
        }

        let left = 0;
        let right = nums.length - 1;

        while (left < right) {
            const mid = left + Math.floor((right - left) / 2);

            if (nums[mid] > nums[right]) {
                left = mid + 1;
            } else {
                right = mid;
            }
        }

        const rotation = left;
        left = 0;
        right = nums.length - 1;

        while (left <= right) {
            const mid = left + Math.floor((right - left) / 2);
            const realIndex = (mid + rotation) % nums.length;

            if (nums[realIndex] === target) {
                return realIndex;
            }

            if (nums[realIndex] < target) {
                left = mid + 1;
            } else {
                right = mid - 1;
            }
        }

        return -1;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(log n)` for finding the rotation and `O(log n)` for the search, which is still `O(log n)` overall.
Space complexity: `O(1)`.

The rotation only breaks the order at one place. Because of that, one half around `mid` is always sorted. The code checks that half first, then decides whether the target can be inside its value range.

For example, with `[4, 5, 6, 7, 0, 1, 2]` and target `0`, the first middle value is `7`. The left side is sorted, but `0` is not in its range, so the left side is removed. The next checks stay in the right part and find index `4`.

```cpp
class Solution{
public:
    int search(vector<int> &nums, int target){
        int left = 0;
        int right = static_cast<int>(nums.size()) - 1;

        while (left <= right) {
            int mid = left + (right - left) / 2;

            if (nums[mid] == target) {
                return mid;
            }

            if (nums[left] <= nums[mid]) {
                if (nums[left] <= target && target < nums[mid]) {
                    right = mid - 1;
                } else {
                    left = mid + 1;
                }
            } else {
                if (nums[mid] < target && target <= nums[right]) {
                    left = mid + 1;
                } else {
                    right = mid - 1;
                }
            }
        }

        return -1;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(log n)` because one half is discarded each time.
Space complexity: `O(1)`. The input array is not changed.

