## Intuition
Linear search checks one element at a time, which is O(N). But here the array is sorted in ascending order. That is a very strong hint.

If we pick any middle element `mid`:

All elements to the left of `mid` are `<= nums[mid]`
All elements to the right of` mid` are `>= nums[mid]`
So by comparing `nums[mid]` with `target`, we can say for sure which half can never contain the answer.

If `nums[mid] == target`, we found it. Return `mid`.
If `nums[mid] > target`, target can only be on the left, because the right side has only larger values. Do `high = mid - 1.`
If `nums[mid] < target`, target can only be on the right, because the left side has only smaller values. Do `low = mid + 1`.

## Approach
The code maintains a search interval from low to high and checks its midpoint on every iteration. If nums[mid] is smaller than the target, it discards the left half; if it is larger, it discards the right half. Because the array is sorted, these discarded portions cannot contain the target. The loop terminates when the target is found or the interval becomes empty, returning -1 in the latter case. This is the optimal approach for a sorted array when random access is available.

